  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "First release. TableMigration: add, drop, rename and change columns with every row converted in one commit, add and drop indexes, rename a table, and plan any of it before applying it. ToSchema brings a table to a schema, such as an entity's (TableMigration.For(User.Model.Schema)): what keeps every value is done, what could lose one (a type change, a narrowing, a column the schema lacks unless dropColumns) is a problem of the plan. Handles stay valid across every migration. Migrations fixed in code: [Migration] classes with snapshots of the model, a generated base whose converters and initializers are abstract and whose transforms and AfterAsync are virtual, declared renames, and a DataLoss level the build checks (LDB2001 to LDB2012). Migrator.MigrateAsync applies them in order, one commit each, records them in the database, refuses a migration that would lose more than it declares (a removed column needs ColumnDrop, a removed table TableDrop), and stops before a migration that may lose more than the run allows. Each entity database has a chain of its own ([Migration(Database = typeof(Db))], LDB2013), and MigrationSet.Leaving keeps tables the chain does not manage out of it. A database without entities is versioned by hand: VersionedMigration classes (Version, Loss and Tables required; Renames, Values and AfterAsync to override) chained with MigrationSet.Chain, trusted as declared.",
